      "problem": "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int 6 \\,\\textrm d x.$",
      "worked_solution": "We can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 6\\,\\text{d}x & =6\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\int {x}^{0}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{0+1}}{0+1}+C \\\\  & =6\\cdot x+C \\\\  & =6x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, we can see that for any constant $k,$ we have $\\displaystyle \\int k \\,\\textrm d x = kx + C.$",
